    <a href="/blog/" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 text-sm mb-4 inline-block">&larr; Back to Blog</a>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-2">Post 1: Teaching Engineering To High Schoolers</h1>
    <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mb-6">February 26, 2019</p>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">
      <p>This last summer I got permission to take a couple weeks off of my research job at Walus labs in order to teach an engineering summer camp to high school students. In this blog post I would like to share my experiences, and my teaching plan for those who are interested in teaching or passionate about engineering.</p>

      <p>To start I want the reader to know that I cherished the experience of teaching the students. It brought me back to when I was their age. Additionally, I enjoyed the opportunity to interact with kids who were interested in learning engineering from me. Overall, I was impressed with how they soaked everything up like a sponge.</p>

      <img src="/files/stem-camp/teachers.jpg" alt="Teachers" class="max-w-xs" />

      <p>The camp that I taught for was located in Richmond and operated out of McNair Secondary School in Richmond, BC. I was given the task of creating a two week plan to teach them basic engineering principles on a topic of my choice and to have them present a science fair project for the end of the camp.</p>

      <img src="/files/stem-camp/teaching-plan-2.JPG" alt="Teaching Plan" />
      <p><a href="/files/stem-camp/teaching-plan-2.pdf">Download Here</a></p>

      <p>I opted to create a teaching plan that involved the kids getting hands on experience with the robots. Two weeks before the beginning of the camp I ordered 3 <a href="https://www.digikey.com/en/product-highlight/m/makeblock/mbot-educational-robotics-platform">mbot educational robotics packages</a>. Here is a video of me making and playing around with the mbots a week before the camp.</p>

      <video src="/files/stem-camp/testing-out-mbot.mp4" poster="/files/stem-camp/testing-out-mbot-moment.jpg" width="250" controls preload></video>

      <p>On day one I was extremely excited. I came in on my first day, greeted the kids. I was instructed by the camp to tell the kids about my experience and my research on the first day.</p>

      <p><a href="/files/stem-camp/intro.pptx">Download the Intro Presentation Here</a></p>

      <p>On my second day I brought in the mbots. Getting there early I set them up in a display where they were all moving simultaneously, which got a laugh from the kids.</p>

      <p><a href="/files/stem-camp/engineering-and-technology.pptx">Download the Presentation Here</a></p>

      <p>The day after the students built the robots, I got permission to bring them into the computer lab.</p>

      <p><a href="/files/stem-camp/how-to-start-mbot.pptx">Download the Presentation Here</a></p>

      <p>The rest of the week was dedicated to the kids working towards competing in a competition which I decided to do upon talking to the students instead of further missions. Essentially the competition was a robot sumo wrestling contest.</p>

      <p><a href="/files/stem-camp/competition.pptx">Download the Presentation Here</a></p>

      <p>Below is a video of Team 1's program winning the final.</p>

      <video src="/files/stem-camp/winning.mp4" poster="/files/stem-camp/winning-moment.jpg" width="400" controls preload></video>
    </div>
